| `length`, inside an index, is the length of what is being indexed. It is
| not a reserved word — `RandomCode { length = 12 }` is a field name in
| several module contracts — so it means this only here, and a variable of
| that name is refused instead (`fail_length_binding.code`).
xs = [1, 2, 3]
assert xs[length - 1] = 3
assert xs[length - 3] = 1

| With a second bound it is a range, and the brackets say which ends are
| included — the interval notation, so `[a, b]` takes both and `[a, b)`
| leaves `b` out. The comma is the same one that separates two of anything
| else on one line.
assert xs[0, length) = [1, 2, 3]
assert xs[0, length - 1] = [1, 2, 3]
assert xs[0, length - 1) = [1, 2]
assert xs[1, 2] = [2, 3]
assert xs[1, 2) = [2]
assert xs (0, 2] = [2, 3]
assert xs (0, 2) = [2]
assert xs (0, length) = [2, 3]

| Both bounds clamp rather than fail: a single index past the end already
| answers null, so a range past the end answers the part that is there.
assert xs[0, 99] = [1, 2, 3]
assert xs[0 - 5, 1] = [1, 2]
assert xs[0, 0) = []
assert xs[2, 1) = []
assert xs[9, 9) = []

| Neither operand is disturbed — a range builds a new value, like `+`.
assert xs = [1, 2, 3]

| `length` also answers for a string (characters, not bytes) and an object
| (its field count), since those are what `[]` accepts besides an array.
words = { a = 1, b = 2 }
assert words["b"] = 2
letters = "héllo"

| What it is for: walking a tree with a work stack, which handlers cannot do
| because none may re-enter itself (`fail_handler_self_cycle.code`).
tree = {
    tag = "ul"
    children = [
        { tag = "li", children = [{ tag = "b", children = [] }] }
        { tag = "li", children = [] }
    ]
}
pending = [tree]
count = 0
loop
    if pending = [], break
    top = pending[length - 1]
    pending = pending[0, length - 1) + top.children
    count += 1
assert count = 4
